{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>You</b> — know my sitting and my rising. The reason: a person's movements are various, and all according to a person's need, thought, and desire; therefore the word לרעי is like 'my thought,' and like it: וברעיון לבו. In Aramaic, רעיונך על משכבך סליקו, and the word comes with a prefixed lamed, like: הרגו לאבנר." "And the meaning of מרחוק — from a distant time, like מרחוק ה' נראה לי." "Now the meaning is: before my thought, in the manner of: בטרם אצרך בבטן ידעתיך." "And the reason for adding <b>You</b> — for You alone know the number of all my movements, not a person himself."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 139, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra quotes the Aramaic clause from Daniel 2:28–29 and the phrase from 2 Samuel 3:30; both left in Hebrew script as he quotes them."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:42:07.143478707Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}